desc: ReaScratch
//tags: reascratch scratch granular wsola
//author: ReaScratch
//
// The jog moves a read head through a sample loaded into memory.
// Hann-windowed grains are always played at 1x, so pitch stays the same at any head speed:
//   head still  -> the same grain repeats (freeze / stutter); random jitter of the grain start
//                  (slider22) breaks the periodic buzz, fading out as the head speeds up (slider23)
//   head moving -> grains step through the file (forward / backward / fast)
// WSOLA alignment: each new grain is shifted by up to +-search ms so that its waveform lines up with
// the natural continuation of the grain already playing (removes most phasing / comb filtering).
//
// MIDI (defaults = Pioneer DDJ-FLX4, deck = channel):
//   Note 0x36 = platter touch, CC 0x22 = platter turn (64 = still, 65 = +1 tick, 63 = -1 tick),
//   CC 0x21 = outer ring (fine), Note 0x0B = PLAY/PAUSE (toggles 'Untouched transport'),
//   Note 0x0C = CUE (jump head to the cue position). 720 ticks per revolution at 33 1/3 rpm = 400 ticks/s = 1x.
// Other controllers (settings 6-10 and 12-17, CV; setup page):
//   Jog CC mode: relative 64-centre (FLX4 / most DJ jogs), relative two's complement (1..63 = +, 127..65 = -),
//   relative sign bit (0..63 = +, 64..127 = -), absolute 7-bit (knob position = head position in the file),
//   absolute 14-bit (CC n = MSB, CC n+32 = LSB).
//   Touch note = 0 -> no touch sensor: the head counts as touched while the jog moves and is released
//   automatically after 'Auto release' ms of silence; Auto release = 0 holds (freeze keeps sounding) until the
//   'Release note' button is pressed. 'Preset' (slider1) fills these settings (CV) for known controllers.
// Without a controller (FX window focused): the waveform well has the whole file (top strip) and a zoom around
//   the head (bottom strip, 2 ms of sample per window pixel). Grab the zoom and drag it = scratch (pressed = touched;
//   the grabbed point stays under the cursor, so dragging left moves forward; Shift = 1/4).
//   Click / drag the whole-file strip = search: jump there and hear short 1x bits while dragging; let go = the
//   stop / play setting goes on from there (CDJ needle search / rekordbox). Right-click either strip = set the cue there.
//   Look (user's choice after mock-ups, 2026-10-03, after iZotope Ozone 12): mirrored hills (linear peak height) in
//   TRACKMENTO mustard (#E6B731, from trackmento.com's CSS) for the played part and grey for the rest; a flat 30 %
//   fill, bright outlines whose inner edges always sit on a whole pixel row (an edge straddling rows showed as an
//   uneven line along a flat stretch), a glow fading outward only. Not smoothed (the user wants the dips to show).
//   Platter (right column, always shown): grab the record and turn it = scratch (pressed = touched; clockwise = forward,
//   1 turn = 1.8 s of sample as at 33 1/3 rpm, Shift = 1/4). The sticker (a row of small arrow heads) shows the read head's angle.
//   (Idea: a test user's Lua 'virtual turntable', 2026-10-03; redrawn here so no extra script or slider is needed.)
//   Keys: Space = PLAY/PAUSE, C = CUE, Shift+C = set the cue at the head, hold X = touched (freeze), hold Z = cut.
// Crossfader cut (sliders 33, 36-37; settings 34-35 (CV)): this deck sits on one side of the crossfader (FLX4: deck 1 = left, ch 7 CC 31/63).
//   'Cut-in' = how far from the closed end the sound starts (lag); 'Fade width' = distance over which it then
//   opens fully (0 = instant on/off, for chirps / transforms). Hold Z (FX window focused) = cut.
//   Slider 38 (crossfader gain) is read by the video sync script, which hides the video while the sound is cut.
// Slider order (the declarations below, from 1): 1 preset | 2-5 sample & playback | 11 head smoothing | 18-23 grain
// sound | 24-31 hidden status (read by the video sync script and tests) | 32 lamp colour | 33, 36-37 crossfader |
// 38-39 hidden crossfader status | 40 quantize | 41 hidden GUI scale (whole-number zoom of the picture) | 42-45 hot cue
// positions | 49 hold time | then the groups the notes below name. The MIDI settings (6-10, 12-17, 34-35, 46-48 ...)
// are not sliders but settings (CV[n], setup page).
// Hot cues (sliders 42-45, 49 and 110-113; pad settings 46-48 (CV); rekordbox-like plus hold-to-preview; user's spec, 2026-10-03):
//   empty pad = set it at the read head; set pad = jump there and play. Released within 'hold time' = keep playing
//   from there; held longer = on release go back to where the head was when the pad was pressed (and to the
//   play / stop state of then), unless PLAY was pressed while holding (then it keeps playing, like a CDJ).
//   SHIFT + pad = clear. Keys: 1-8 = pads, Shift+1-8 = clear (8 since 2026-10-05, ⚪36: sliders 110-113 = 5-8). FLX4 pads in HOT CUE mode: deck 1 = ch 8 notes 0-7
//   (SHIFT ch 9), deck 2 = ch 10 (SHIFT ch 11) (Mixxx mapping). Positions are sliders, so they are saved with the project.
// Slider 2 is the sample file (token 2 on the chunk line). Renumbering sliders shifts saved values: migrate chunks.
// Pitch mode (slider 52, a test user's idea; the user chose a bar from 'pitch' to 'tape', 2026-10-03): how far the pitch
//   follows the speed. One stream of grains whose read rate is sign(v) |v|^a (v = head speed, a = the bar): 0 = the
//   pitch-locked grains above, 1 = tape (the speed is the pitch, like a record; held still = silent), 0.5 = half the
//   octaves (2x speed = +6 semitones, 0.25x = -12). Until 2026-10-03 the in-between values mixed the grains and a
//   separate tape read, which sounded like two copies and cancelled out near 0.25 (user's report).
//   With a > 0 the grains follow the head's motion sample by sample and sit (1 - a) x half a grain behind it, so at 1
//   every grain reads the head itself and they add up to the plain tape read; the WSOLA search and the jitter shrink
//   to none at 1. At 1x speed every value plays the file as it is. A jump of the head (CUE, hot cue, loop) fades the
//   grains out over 5 ms where they are and fades a full set in at the new place, so it does not click or smear.
//   The direction follows the motion for any a > 0 (only 0 has the 'Grain direction' choice).
//   Tape quality (2026-10-03): the head must move without jitter, because in tape its
//   speed is the pitch. Relative jog ticks are applied at their own sample offset (not all at the block start) and the
//   head follows the target through three one-pole stages (each half of 'Head smoothing'), so the speed is continuous and
//   passes smoothly through zero when the hand reverses. The mouse goal (one per GUI frame) is reached at a steady rate
//   over one frame interval instead of a glide. The following grains read with 6-point Lagrange interpolation, and a 12 dB/oct
//   low cut follows the read rate (5 Hz at 1x and faster, up to 50 Hz near standstill), so slow motion and reversals do
//   not rumble while normal playback keeps its bass.
// Spin on release (slider 53; user's request after trying a rewind on the Reloop Buddy, whose small jog stops at once,
//   2026-10-03): when the hand leaves the platter (touch off, mouse up) while the head moves at least 1x away from
//   where the transport goes (1x when 'play', still when 'stop'), the head keeps that speed and is pulled back to it
//   at a constant rate, like a record released mid-spin with the motor on (a linear brake, as cdj3k-emu measured on
//   the CDJ jog). The slider is the time to lose 1.8x (one turn per second); 0 = off. Touching again, CUE, a hot cue
//   or a search ends the spin.
// CUE button (CUE note / C key), CDJ style (user's choice, 2026-10-03): playing -> back to the cue position and stop;
//   stopped -> the cue position becomes the head position (if it is elsewhere) and plays while the button is held;
//   letting go goes back to the cue and stops, unless PLAY was pressed while holding (then it keeps playing). Shift+C
//   still sets the cue without playing. Hot cues: right-click a hot cue line on the waveform = clear it.
// Slip (slider 54, off by default; rekordbox / CDJ SLIP, user's spec 2026-10-03): while a hand scratches (jog, platter,
//   waveform drag, X key) or a hot cue pad is held past the hold time, playback goes on silently in the background
//   (slip_pos, 1x from where the head was); letting go puts the head there and it plays on, as if nothing had happened.
//   Only when it was playing at the press: from stop everything is as without slip (a held hot cue goes back to where
//   the head was). A short tap on a hot cue, or PLAY while holding it, still means "go on from here". With slip the
//   release does not spin (slider 53). CUE and a search end it. The background place shows as a pale line on the
//   waveform. Switch: the lit 'slip' push button at the top right of the frame, the S key, or the setup page (Hot cue tab).
// Brake / start / spinback (sliders 57-60, settings 61-62 (CV), user's choices 2026-10-04): PLAY to stop lets the head slow down from its
//   speed to still at a constant rate ('Brake on stop' = s from 1x, like a turntable's brake), PLAY to start speeds it
//   up from still to 1x ('Start on play'); both off by default (instant, as before). Only the PLAY button (MIDI / Space)
//   brakes and starts: CUE, hot cues, memory cues and the stop at the end stay instant. Spinback (B key or its MIDI
//   note) throws the head backward at 'Spinback speed' and lets it slow down to still over 'Spinback time', then it
//   stays stopped. They share the spin on release: the head is pulled to the transport's speed at a constant rate
//   (spin_r); touching the platter, CUE, a hot cue or a search ends it. The sound follows the pitch bar (slider 52) as
//   any motion does: pitch-locked grains slow down without a pitch drop; the tape end gives the falling pitch.
// Brake in beats / PAUSE quantize (sliders 232-233, ⚪83, user's request 2026-10-08, from a vinyl break trick: PLAY / PAUSE
//   pressed on the beat over a short loop): 'Brake on stop in beats' > 0 and a BPM = the brake takes that many beats of
//   this deck's beat at the speed it plays (tempo and sync included) instead of the seconds of slider 57. 'PAUSE
//   quantize' (Quantize on): PLAY to stop waits for the next 1 / 1/2 / 1/4 beat and the brake starts there; the grid is
//   the other deck's beat while it plays (as the quantized PLAY), else this deck's own grid by the head (in a loop shorter
//   than the step it stops where the loop goes round). Pressed up to Q_LATE s after a step = at once; PLAY again while it
//   waits = keep playing; a touch, a search or a stop some other way drops it; Quantize off stops at once.
// Quantize (slider 40, BPM 63, beat grid 64; user's choices 2026-10-04): while playing, a hot cue, a memory cue CALL,
//   the way back from a held hot cue and CUE wait for the next beat of the sample (beats = slider 64 + k * 60 / BPM)
//   and jump then, landing at the same place in the beat. Pressed up to Q_LATE s after a beat = jump at once, a little
//   way into the cue so the beat stays in place. Stopped, touched or searching = at once, as before. Slider 40 held
//   'Platter: Hidden / Shown' before (the record is always shown now), so its values are On = 0, Off = 1: older
//   projects saved 1 and open with quantize off. BPM and the grid come from the import script (estimated), the T key
//   (tap on the beat while playing; Shift+T = this place is a beat) or the setup page. Switch: Q key, setup (Beat tab).
// Samples (sliders 65-72, settings 73-74 (CV), user's choices 2026-10-04): up to 8 samples, slot 1 = slider 2, slots 2-8 = sliders 65-71.
//   All of them are loaded into memory one after another (the switch is instant; together they share the memory, about
//   23 min stereo 48 kHz). Slider 72 = the slot in use. Each slot keeps its own read head position (back where it was
//   left), CUE, hot cues, memory cues, BPM and beat grid: on a switch the sliders of the slot left are stored and those of
//   the new slot put in (kept with the project by @serialize). The transport (stop / play) stays as it is. With slip on, the
//   samples not in use play on in the background while the transport plays (back = where it would be now; user's choice B). Switch: F1-F8,
//   the 1-8 buttons in the bottom bar, MIDI notes (setting 73 (CV) = channel, 74 = note of slot 1; FLX4 SAMPLER mode pads,
//   deck 1 = ch 8, deck 2 = ch 10, notes 48-55). An empty slot is not switched to (except by setting slider 72, as the
//   import script does). The video sync script puts slot k's video at (k - 1) x 3600 s on the timeline.
//   Sliders above 64 are written on the chunk line after the preset name: slider n > 64 = token n + 1.
//   Slider 75 = 'forget slot k's cues' (the import script sets it after putting a new file in slot k). An empty slot points
//   at '(empty).wav' (no samples): with a missing default REAPER picked the first file in the folder for every slot.
// Deck B (sliders 76-80, 2026-10-04): a second copy of this JSFX on "scratch engine B" (set up by reascratch_setup.lua)
//   is deck B: slider 76 = 2. Each deck writes its crossfader gain into the shared memory ReaScratch_video (gmem[0] deck A,
//   gmem[1] deck B); deck A also writes the video blend (gmem[2], slider 78) and the key threshold / softness (gmem[3-4],
//   sliders 79-80). A Video processor on the "video" track reads them and mixes the two decks' videos
//   (Scripts/ReaScratch/reascratch_deck_mix.eel). Slider 77 = fade curve: linear or equal power (sin; user's choice,
//   the middle keeps the loudness). Hidden from the front page; setup page: Crossfader tab (curve), Video tab (blend).
// Layout (slider 81, setup page 'Layout' tab; user's spec, 2026-10-04): right-handed = the record on the right, as before;
//   left-handed = the front page mirrored, like the left half of a DJ controller seen from deck B's: the record on the
//   left, each group of buttons / readouts at its mirrored place (a group keeps its inside order: hot cues 1-4, the
//   sample buttons, the readouts), the grain knobs mirrored one by one with their names and wires; knobs still turn and
//   switches still throw the same way. Any deck, also with one deck; the setup script makes a new deck B left-handed.
// Video FX (sliders 82-87, setting 88 (CV), setup page 'FX' tab; user's choice from Resolume's effects, 2026-10-04): each deck puts an
//   effect on its own picture (82 = which, 83 = amount, turned by a centred knob: slider 84 channel / 85 CC, centre =
//   off, either way = more; FLX4 CFX knob: ch 7 CC 23 / 24, not yet tried on the hardware), and deck A's sliders 86-87
//   put one on the mixed picture (setting 88 (CV) = a CC on the same channel, 0..127 = amount). The deck writes into the shared
//   memory: [10..13] deck A / [20..23] deck B = effect, amount, beat position (beats from the grid, -1 = no BPM),
//   lamp colour (the tint); [5] / [6] = the master effect and amount. Drawn by the deck mix Video processor.
// Tempo and SYNC (sliders 89-91, settings 92-93 (CV), user's choices 2026-10-04): slider 89 = tempo (%), the transport's speed is 1 + it / 100
//   ('1x' of play, slip, brake / start, spin back to the transport, the background play of the other samples); the
//   video follows the read head, so it stretches with the sound. The pitch always stays (user's choice): the pitch bar
//   (slider 52) works on the speed relative to the tempo, so playing at the tempo sounds at the file's pitch in any
//   mode, and in tape mode a tempo other than 0 % turns the WSOLA search back on. Slider 90 = the range of the tempo
//   slider (10 choices: +-8 / 16 / 50 % or +-5 .. 60 BPM, default +-20 BPM); MIDI: setting 92 (CV) = the tempo fader's CC on the deck channel (CC+32 = LSB, the
//   value 0 = fastest as on the FLX4: ch 1 / 2 CC 0 / 32; -1 = none); setting 93 (CV) = the SYNC note (FLX4 BEAT SYNC 0x58),
//   that note + 8 = next tempo range (FLX4 SHIFT+BEAT SYNC 0x60). A fader that disagrees with the tempo (after SYNC, the
//   keys, a range change, a reload) is ignored until it reaches the tempo (soft takeover); then it rules and SYNC goes off.
//   SYNC (slider 91, rekordbox BEAT SYNC): each deck writes into the shared memory its BPM at the tempo ([14] deck A /
//   [24] deck B, 0 = no BPM) and a block counter ([15] / [25], alive); with SYNC on the tempo follows the other deck's BPM
//   (half / double time when that is nearer to 0 %, at most +-50 %), and pressing SYNC (or PLAY while SYNC is on) moves
//   the head to the same place in the beat as the other deck (its beat position [12] / [22]; up to one audio block late).
//   Moving the tempo by hand (fader, keys, mouse) turns SYNC off. The BPM readout shows the BPM at the tempo.
//   Keys: Up / Down = tempo +-0.1 %, Page Up / Down = +-1 %, Y = SYNC, Shift+Y = range. Mouse: the tempo readout on the frame
//   (drag / wheel; double- or right-click = 0 %), the sync button beside it.
// CPU meter and load test (sliders 94-97, 2026-10-04, ⚪19): 94 = the worst block of the last second (% of the block's
//   time), 95 = the average (% of real time), 96 = the CPU time of the last sample load (ms, spread over blocks, see
//   sm_load), 97 = load test: the deck scratches by itself (reascratch_load_test.lua turns it on; always off at start-up).
// Dropout counter (sliders 98-99, 2026-10-04, ⚪19): REAPER's GetUnderrunTime stayed 0 with WaveOut and ASIO (Fireface)
//   through a 0.3-0.6 s stall of the audio thread, so the JSFX counts by itself: how far @block has fallen behind real
//   time (wall clock minus the audio played, against its recent lowest). Behind by more than the threshold = one
//   dropout (99 counts up; then the count starts from there). 98 = threshold in ms (0 = one block + 3 ms; the load test
//   script sets the device buffer + 3 ms). Needs the track to run in real time (armed + monitored, as the setup makes it).
// Reloop Buddy (settings 100-107 (CV), 2026-10-05, ⚪33; measured on the hardware): a 10-bit tempo fader (CC9 = high 3 bits,
//   then CC63 = low 7: setting 100), SHIFT+SYNC = SYNC note + 1 (101), pad modes kept here, as djay does - holding the
//   mode button (deck channel note 32: 102) a pad sends a mode note on the pad channel (103 HOT CUE, 104 SAMPLER; other
//   modes = the pads do nothing), SHIFT+pad = pad note + 8 on the same channel (105), a channel volume fader (106: sound
//   and picture), FX ON buttons (107: this deck's video FX on / off) and one FX knob (88 on deck A = the master's
//   amount; CC 0, so 85 / 88 say none with -1).
// Swing (slider 109, 2026-10-05, ⚪34; user's choice for a mashup): the transport's 1x play (and the slip background
//   play) runs each 16th at its own speed so the off-beat 16th lands late: 50 % = straight, 58 % = the measured groove
//   of the test song, 67 % = triplet. Read on the deck's beat grid (BPM + Beat grid): the first 16th of each eighth
//   at 0.5 / s, the second at 0.5 / (1 - s) of the speed, so the eighths stay where they were (SYNC keeps lining up);
//   the pitch does not move (grains), the video follows the head. No BPM = straight.
// Start-up: the stop / play switch (slider 3) is always on stop when a project opens or the FX loads, whatever was saved.
// NOTE: EEL2 identifiers are case-insensitive (G and g are the same variable) - keep names distinct.

options:maxmem=134217728 gmem=ReaScratch_video

slider1:0<0,133,1{Custom,⚠ APC40 mkI deck 1,⚠ APC40 mkI deck 2,APC40 mkII deck 1,APC40 mkII deck 2,⚠ Behringer CMD Micro deck 1,⚠ Behringer CMD Micro deck 2,⚠ Denon SC5000/SC6000 deck 1,⚠ Denon SC5000/SC6000 deck 2,⚠ Denon Prime 4 deck 1,⚠ Denon Prime 4 deck 2,⚠ Gemini FirstMix deck 1,⚠ Gemini FirstMix deck 2,⚠ Hercules DJ Control MP3 e2 deck 1,⚠ Hercules DJ Control MP3 e2 deck 2,⚠ Hercules DJControl Compact deck 1,⚠ Hercules DJControl Compact deck 2,⚠ Hercules DJControl Starlight deck 1,⚠ Hercules DJControl Starlight deck 2,⚠ Hercules DJControl MIX deck 1,⚠ Hercules DJControl MIX deck 2,⚠ Hercules DJ Control Instinct deck 1,⚠ Hercules DJ Control Instinct deck 2,⚠ Hercules DJ Control AIR deck 1,⚠ Hercules DJ Control AIR deck 2,⚠ Hercules DJ Console Mk4 deck 1,⚠ Hercules DJ Console Mk4 deck 2,⚠ Hercules DJ Console 4-Mx deck 1,⚠ Hercules DJ Console 4-Mx deck 2,⚠ Hercules DJ 4Set deck 1,⚠ Hercules DJ 4Set deck 2,⚠ Hercules DJControl Jogvision deck 1,⚠ Hercules DJControl Jogvision deck 2,⚠ Hercules Inpulse 200/300 (MK2) deck 1,⚠ Hercules Inpulse 200/300 (MK2) deck 2,⚠ Hercules Inpulse 500 deck 1,⚠ Hercules Inpulse 500 deck 2,⚠ ION Discover DJ deck 1,⚠ ION Discover DJ deck 2,⚠ ION Discover DJ Pro deck 1,⚠ ION Discover DJ Pro deck 2,⚠ Korg KAOSS DJ deck 1,⚠ Korg KAOSS DJ deck 2,⚠ HID bridge (NI S2/S3/S4) deck 1,⚠ HID bridge (NI S2/S3/S4) deck 2,⚠ Numark Party Mix deck 1,⚠ Numark Party Mix deck 2,⚠ Numark Party Mix II/Live deck 1,⚠ Numark Party Mix II/Live deck 2,⚠ Numark DJ2GO2 deck 1,⚠ Numark DJ2GO2 deck 2,⚠ Numark DJ2GO2 Touch deck 1,⚠ Numark DJ2GO2 Touch deck 2,⚠ Numark Mixtrack deck 1,⚠ Numark Mixtrack deck 2,⚠ Numark Mixtrack Pro deck 1,⚠ Numark Mixtrack Pro deck 2,⚠ Numark Mixtrack 2 deck 1,⚠ Numark Mixtrack 2 deck 2,⚠ Numark Mixtrack (Pro) 3 deck 1,⚠ Numark Mixtrack (Pro) 3 deck 2,⚠ Numark Mixtrack Platinum deck 1,⚠ Numark Mixtrack Platinum deck 2,⚠ Numark Mixtrack Pro/Platinum FX deck 1,⚠ Numark Mixtrack Pro/Platinum FX deck 2,⚠ DDJ-FLX2 deck 1,⚠ DDJ-FLX2 deck 2,⚠ DDJ-FLX4 deck 1,⚠ DDJ-FLX4 deck 2,⚠ DDJ-FLX6 deck 1,⚠ DDJ-FLX6 deck 2,⚠ DDJ-FLX10 deck 1,⚠ DDJ-FLX10 deck 2,⚠ DDJ-REV1 deck 1,⚠ DDJ-REV1 deck 2,⚠ DDJ-REV5 deck 1,⚠ DDJ-REV5 deck 2,⚠ DDJ-200 deck 1,⚠ DDJ-200 deck 2,⚠ DDJ-RB deck 1,⚠ DDJ-RB deck 2,⚠ DDJ-400 deck 1,⚠ DDJ-400 deck 2,⚠ DDJ-800 deck 1,⚠ DDJ-800 deck 2,⚠ DDJ-1000 deck 1,⚠ DDJ-1000 deck 2,⚠ DDJ-SB deck 1,⚠ DDJ-SB deck 2,⚠ DDJ-SB2 deck 1,⚠ DDJ-SB2 deck 2,⚠ DDJ-SB3 / SX deck 1,⚠ DDJ-SB3 / SX deck 2,⚠ DDJ-SX3 deck 1,⚠ DDJ-SX3 deck 2,⚠ CDJ-2000NXS2 (MIDI ch 1),⚠ CDJ-2000NXS2 (MIDI ch 2),⚠ CDJ-3000/3000X (MIDI ch 1),⚠ CDJ-3000/3000X (MIDI ch 2),⚠ XDJ-RX3 / XZ deck 1,⚠ XDJ-RX3 / XZ deck 2,Reloop Buddy deck 1,Reloop Buddy deck 2,⚠ Reloop Ready deck 1,⚠ Reloop Ready deck 2,⚠ Reloop Mixage deck 1,⚠ Reloop Mixage deck 2,⚠ Reloop Mixon 4 deck 1,⚠ Reloop Mixon 4 deck 2,⚠ Reloop Mixon 8 Pro deck 1,⚠ Reloop Mixon 8 Pro deck 2,⚠ Reloop Touch deck 1,⚠ Reloop Touch deck 2,⚠ Reloop Beatpad deck 1,⚠ Reloop Beatpad deck 2,⚠ Reloop Beatpad 2 deck 1,⚠ Reloop Beatpad 2 deck 2,⚠ Reloop Beatmix 2/4 deck 1,⚠ Reloop Beatmix 2/4 deck 2,⚠ Reloop Terminal Mix 2/4 deck 1,⚠ Reloop Terminal Mix 2/4 deck 2,⚠ Reloop Terminal Mix 8 deck 1,⚠ Reloop Terminal Mix 8 deck 2,⚠ DVS (timecode vinyl / CD) deck 1,⚠ DVS (timecode vinyl / CD) deck 2,⚠ Pioneer DJ PLX-CRSS12 (DVS) deck 1,⚠ Pioneer DJ PLX-CRSS12 (DVS) deck 2,⚠ Reloop RP-8000 MK2 (DVS) deck 1,⚠ Reloop RP-8000 MK2 (DVS) deck 2,⚠ Rane Twelve / Twelve MKII deck 1,⚠ Rane Twelve / Twelve MKII deck 2,⚠ Reloop RP-8000 (DVS) deck 1,⚠ Reloop RP-8000 (DVS) deck 2,FLX4 Emu test (loopMIDI deck 2)}>-Preset
